  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot temperatures on the way today. \n\nThere is a good chance today brings that last shot of >90 F \ntemperatures until next year. This heat will be fueled by an \nanomalously strong and amplified ridge over the Southeast CONUS \nand sfc high pressure near Bermuda. Forecast highs are around 90\nF for mainland VA and NC and in the mid-upper 80s for the \nEastern Shore. A southwesterly wind will tend to keep even those\nat the immediate coast of SE VA nearly as warm as those well \ninland. A well-mixed boundary layer also should keep any \nappreciable humidity at bay, with dew points this afternoon in \nthe lower 60s. Records are generally out of reach but have been \nincluded in the 'Climate' section below for reference. Mostly \nsunny skies prevail with a modest increase in higher clouds late\nin the afternoon, especially across the nrn half of the \nforecast area. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled weather pattern with cooler and wetter \nconditions is likely this weekend, followed by a gradual drying \ntrend and cool temperatures next week.\n\nAn initial cold front will approach the northern portions of the our \narea later today into tonight. The associated trough aloft is very \nflat, so this front won't have much vigor to it. Regardless, a few \nshowers are likely as early as this evening and early tonight as the \nfront gradually pushes S, mostly across the northern tier. \nThere is a better chance at more widespread (anafrontal) rain \nduring the day Saturday, with the highest PoPs (60-70%) in a \ncorridor from the Piedmont through the Richmond Metro and \nNorthern Neck. PoPs taper off to 20-40% across SE VA and NE NC, \nthough will ramp up overnight Saturday. Given the widespread \ncloudiness and precip, have undercut the NBM highs by a few \ndegrees. This yields high temps in the mid- upper 60s for most \nof our VA counties, except around 70 F near the NC/VA border. \nWarmest across NE NC and especially near the Albemarle Sound \nwhere mid- upper 70s are forecast. \n\nThe bulk of the QPF and more widespread overrunning rainfall arrive \nSaturday night through Monday morning ahead of a second cold front, \nwhich is anticipated on Monday. There continues to be model \nagreement that Sunday will be wet with widespread showers/rain as \ntropical moisture streams into the area between the cold fronts. The \ninitial batch of widespread precip likely moves in from the SW after \nmidnight Saturday into early Sunday morning, with categorical (80-\n90%) PoPs spreading E/NE through the day Sunday. A widespread 1-1.5\" \nis still expected for most of the area, though there remains some \ndifferences among the guidance on where the corridor of highest \ntotals will set up. In general, there has been a southward \nadjustment in the highest totals, now in the southern VA into \nNE NC areas. These locations could see localized totals up to \n2\". Totals gradually decrease with northward extent. The chance \nfor thunderstorms remains quite low due to cool sfc temps, but a\nbrief increase in CAPE across the far SE Sunday afternoon could\nallow for an embedded storm or two. Speaking of temps: again \ncut a few degrees off the NBM guidance, with highs ranging from \nthe mid-upper 60s NW to lower 70s SE.\n\nA strong area of cool high pressure builds in behind the front, \nresulting in cooler and drier conditions for next week. Temps will \nbe at or even below seasonal norms with overnight lows dipping into \nthe 40s inland by mid week. There is even some potential for upper \n30s, as suggested by some of the statistical models.",
